  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 258 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nUpper level ridging and surface high pressure will keep dry \nconditions around through the weekend. A gradual pattern shift is \nexpected by Sunday evening as a series of troughs pivot through \nthrough Nebraska and the central Plains allowing for near daily \nafternoon/evening thunderstorms Sunday through Tuesday. At this time \nthe severe risk remains uncertain, but would not be surprised if a \nstronger storm or two develops. Otherwise, the only other major \nconcern in the extended period will be the increasing temperatures \nthrough Monday. Highs will generally rise into the low 90s east of a \nBrady to Valentine line. To the west of this line, highs are \nexpected to rise into the upper 90s to low 100s. There is some \nconcern that these highs have the potential to achieve over 100 \ndegrees by the end of the weekend. Latest probability forecasts show \nthe potential of seeing over 100 degrees to be 40 to 50 percent for \nmuch of southwest Nebraska into the Panhandle. Elsewhere, \nprobabilities are still near 30 percent for much of the western half\nof the forecast area. At this time, while high temperatures will\nbe very hot, heat indicies will remain near or below 100 degrees.\nHowever, if this warming trend continues and confidence increases\nover the next few days, heat headlines may be necessary. Warm \ntemperatures continue into Monday, although, they won't be quite as\nhigh as the weekend as the overall pattern slowly shifts and some\ncooler air will begin working into northern Nebraska. Expect a range\nfrom the upper 80s across north central to the upper 90s in \nsouthwest Nebraska on Monday. Temperatures fall back to near or \nslightly below normals on Tuesday ranging from the low 80s in north\ncentral to the low 90s in southwest Nebraska.",
